1020 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 137 0 R
/T (If the items were kept in a linked list of dynamically allocated structures \(each node holding an item and a next pointer\): add at the head is O\(1\) \(an array adds at the end in O\(1\) while there is room, but has a fixed maximum, 10 here\). Remove is O\(n\) to find the node and then O\(1\) to unlink it, while the array needs O\(n\) to find it and O\(n\) to shift the elements. Search by id is O\(n\) in both. The array allows fast access by index and is stored in one block, which is good for sorting; the list has no fixed limit and removal does not shift data. Extra space: each node needs one next pointer \(8 bytes on a 64-bit system\), plus the memory manager's bookkeeping, so 10 items need at least 80 extra bytes. Sorting a list by swapping nodes is more difficult. For a small canteen menu \(10 items\) the array of structures is simpler and good enough.)
/E (If the items were kept in a linked list of dynamically allocated structures \(each node holding an item and a next pointer\): add at the head is O\(1\) \(an array adds at the end in O\(1\) while there is room, but has a fixed maximum, 10 here\). Remove is O\(n\) to find the node and then O\(1\) to unlink it, while the array needs O\(n\) to find it and O\(n\) to shift the elements. Search by id is O\(n\) in both. The array allows fast access by index and is stored in one block, which is good for sorting; the list has no fixed limit and removal does not shift data. Extra space: each node needs one next pointer \(8 bytes on a 64-bit system\), plus the memory manager's bookkeeping, so 10 items need at least 80 extra bytes. Sorting a list by swapping nodes is more difficult. For a small canteen menu \(10 items\) the array of structures is simpler and good enough.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
